<<<
Ultramontanism. jardinière. For pronunciation see FRENCH WORDS.
===
Ultramontanism.

**jardinière.** For pronunciation see ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
jargon is perhaps the most variously applied
===
**jargon** is perhaps the most variously applied
>>>

<<<
The words are: argot, cant, dialect, gibberish, idiom, jargon, lingo, parlance, patois, shop, slang, vernacular.
===
The words are: *argot, cant, dialect, gibberish, idiom, jargon, lingo, parlance, patois, shop, slang, vernacular.*
>>>

<<<
worth: dialect, idiom, & parlance, are Greek
===
worth: *dialect, idiom,* & *parlance,* are Greek
>>>

<<<
(dialegomai I talk; idios private or proper or peculiar; paraballo compare);
===
(*dialegomai* I talk; *idios* private or proper or peculiar; *paraballo* compare);
>>>

<<<
cant & vernacular are Latin
===
*cant* & *vernacular* are Latin
>>>

<<<
(cantus song, chant, whine; verna homeborn slave);
===
(*cantus* song, chant, whine; *verna* homeborn slave);
>>>

<<<
lingo is Italian (lingua franca Frankish tongue);
===
*lingo* is Italian (*lingua franca* Frankish tongue);
>>>

<<<
argot, jargon, & patois, are French
===
*argot, jargon,* & *patois,* are French
>>>

<<<
gibberish & shop & slang are English
===
*gibberish* & *shop* & *slang* are English
>>>

<<<
argot is primarily the vocabulary
===
*argot* is primarily the vocabulary
>>>

<<<
cant in current English means the insincere
===
*cant* in current English means the insincere
>>>

<<<
dialect is essentially local; a d. is the variety of a language that prevails in a district, with local peculiarities of vocabulary, pronunciation, & phrase. gibberish is the name for unintelligible stuff:
===
*dialect* is essentially local; *a d.* is the variety of a language that prevails in a district, with local peculiarities of vocabulary, pronunciation, & phrase.

*gibberish* is the name for unintelligible stuff:
>>>

<<<
(for which, as a familiar term, lingo is better)
===
(for which, as a familiar term, *lingo* is better)
>>>

<<<
idiom is the method of expression characteristic of or peculiar to the native speakers of a language; i.c., it is racy or unaffected or natural English (or French &e.), especially
===
*idiom* is the method of expression characteristic of or peculiar to the native speakers of a language; i.e., it is racy or unaffected or natural English (or French &c.), especially
>>>

<<<
prevalent in other languages; & an i. is a particular example of such speech. An earlier sense, the same as that of dialect, still occurs sometimes. See also IDIOM.
===
prevalent in other languages; & *an i.* is a particular example of such speech. An earlier sense, the same as that of *dialect*, still occurs sometimes. See also ^^IDIOM^^.
>>>

<<<
jargon is talk that is considered
===
*jargon* is talk that is considered
>>>

<<<
(cf. cant, slang); (2) hybrid
===
(cf. *cant, slang*); (2) hybrid
>>>

<<<
lingo is a contemptuous name for any foreign language. It is sometimes used instead of jargon (1) & (2).
===
*lingo* is a contemptuous name for any foreign language. It is sometimes used instead of *jargon* (1) & (2).
>>>

<<<
what is now more often expressed by jargon or slang, the special
===
what is now more often expressed by *jargon* or *slang*, the special
>>>

<<<
parlance, which means manner of speaking,
===
*parlance,* which means manner of speaking,
>>>

<<<
not That is parlance; parlance is always accompanied by an adjective or defining word or phrase, & that adjective, not parlance, gives the point:
===
not That is parlance; *parlance* is always accompanied by an adjective or defining word or phrase, & that adjective, not *parlance*, gives the point:
>>>
